s not the primary one. The stomach is looking for a specific physical state (a liquid, in this case), and that's what dictates whether food moves on, not the clock. The amount of time that a piece of food needs to be digested and reach that state depends on what the food is made of, and it's not the same for all foods.
15:08:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a really logical way to look at it. If every single thing you ate started out with the exact same texture and chemical makeup, then you would be 100% right: consistency would just be a biological stopwatch, and "time in the stomach" would equal "readiness to leave." But the reason it is *not* just time in disguise is that **foods do not start at the same consistency, nor do they break down at the same speed.** Because of this, a newer food can achieve the "ready" consistency much faster than an older food. If the stomach were truly differentiating by time (even in disguise), it would enforce a strict "first-in, first-out" rule. But it doesn't. It measures *state*, not *duration*. Here is a way to visualize the difference: Imagine a bouncer at a club who has one strict rule: **You can only enter if you are wearing a tie.** * **Person A (The Steak)** arrives at 8:00 PM. He is wearing a t-shirt. The bouncer tells him to stand to the side and put on a tie. Because he has to unpack a tie, figure out how to tie it, and get it adjusted, it's going to take him 10 minutes. * **Person B (The Soup)** arrives at 8:05 PM. He is already wearing a perfectly tied tie. The bouncer lets Person B in immediately. The bouncer doesn't care that Person A has been waiting for 5 minutes, and he doesn't care that Person B just walked up 2 seconds ago. The bouncer is not checking a watch; he is only checking for the tie. The stomach works exactly the same way. The pyloric valve (the bouncer) only checks for a liquid state (the tie). If you eat a tough piece of meat, it might take 3 hours of acid and churning to turn it into a liquid. If you drink a glass of milk an hour later, that milk is *already* a liquid. It doesn't need to wait for the meat's 3-hour timer to finish. It slips right past the meat and exits the stomach in minutes. So, while time is required to change the consistency of *solid* foods, the stomach itself has no concept of how long anything has been in there. It only knows what is a liquid right now, and what is not.
